<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Datarpur State</h1><p class="st-meta">1550 CE – 1818 CE (approx.) · capital Datarpur</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Rajput hill principality in the Shivalik foothills of the Punjab, swallowed by Ranjit Singh's Sikh Empire in 1818.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Datarpur was one of the petty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> states of the outer Himalayan hills between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beas">Beas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a>, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoshiarpur-district">Hoshiarpur district</a>. Its ruling family belonged to the Dadwal clan and traced kinship to neighbouring hill houses such as those of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kangra-state">Kangra</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/guler-state">Guler</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is recorded of its internal history. Like its neighbours it was a small chieftaincy of hill villages and forts, paying tribute in turn to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>, to Raja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sansar-chand-katoch">Sansar Chand</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kangra-state">Kangra</a> and then to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhs">Sikhs</a> as their power grew in the late 18th century.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1818 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Maharaja Ranjit Singh</a> annexed Datarpur to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a>, leaving the family a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagir">jagir</a>. The area passed to the British with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bist-doab">Jalandhar Doab</a> in 1846.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/datarpur-state.jpg" alt="Datarpur State" width="320" height="297"><figcaption>Image: Surveyed by Alexander Cunningham, drawn by Kalleemoodden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Detail_of_the_main,_continuous_tract_of_territory_of_Datarpur_from_a_map_of_the_various_Hill_States_of_the_Punjab_Hills_region,_copied_in_1852.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1550 CE – 1818 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Datarpur</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Punjabi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Datarpur</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1684&amp;focus=datarpur-state" aria-label="Datarpur State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6629 1020 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Datarpur State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6949 1276L6918 1285"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6918 1285h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6949 1276h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6949 1276h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1684&amp;focus=datarpur-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

